here are some facts about great white  sharks white sharks are live birth  usually in litters of between four and  seven individuals now they're called  pups but when they're born they're  between 1.2 and 1.5 meters so that's a  pretty big baby  it takes a great white shark about 10 to  12 years to reach maturity at which  point the females are actually about a  metre longer than the males the largest  recorded being over 6 meters roughly the  size of this rent boat binds at this  length they'd weigh about 2 to 3,000  kilograms oh man now there are stories  of some great whites being over 7 meters  but those are unsubstantiated white  sharks are warm bodies they're not  warm-blooded like us they can't maintain  a perfectly stable temperature but their  internal organs are kept at up to 13  degrees above the average temperature of  the ambient water around them the heat  generated by their muscles is actually  transferred to the blood in their veins  as it returns from the Sharks  extremities so it warms it up and keeps  the core temperature a little bit hotter  this allows the shark to venture into  colder waters and also to have explosive  power its muscles work a lot better when  warm than when cold just like ours the  trade-off is the shark requires about 10  times as much energy as if it didn't  heat its body and that's why they need  to feed on these blubber rich seals and  whale carcasses white sharks like to  hunt when it's light out because they  use their eyesight to spot their prey  but when they open their jaws their eyes  actually roll back into their head to  protect them and so they're actually  blind when they're taking a bite now  they do like conditions that are a  little bit low visibility because they  rely on stealth to track down their prey  if a seal spots them coming it's  basically game over because the seal is  so much more maneuverable and can  definitely get away from the shark but  they only eat sea mammals after they're  about 2.5 meters long which is why most  of the Sharks we're seeing around here  are quite large before that they're died  consists mainly of fish the gestation  period is thought to be about 18 months  and that leads to a two to three year  reproduction cycle and with such small  litters that means it takes a long time  for this shark population to recover  there are a couple of misconceptions  about great white sharks one is that  they can't get cancer and that's led to  a lot of people hunting them down and  trying to use their fins as an  anti-cancer soup but in reality sharks  get cancer just like anything else and  there's photographic evidence of sharks  with big tumors so it makes no sense to  hunt down sharks as a cancer remedy  because they get cancer just like we do  another misconception is sharks are  coastal creatures that just cruise the  beaches waiting to bite people in  reality the Sharks spend much of their  time way away out at sea and very deep  over a kilometer deep it's kind of  shocking but we've only found this out  in the last couple of years so there's  so much about sharks that remains  undiscovered we don't know where they go  or what they do for most of the time  that they're alive and that's why  research projects like this are so  important to find out more about the  shark and figure out how we can help it  rehabilitate and and become the predator  of the sea that it once was  you
 

